<p class="lesson-lead">The game changes genre: an item grants capability, a lever fires an event, and a scene load flips gravity. Three lessons that turn your overworld RPG into a platformer — by decoupling cause from effect.</p>

<h2>Lessons</h2>
<table>
    <tr><th>ID</th><th>Title</th><th>What you build / learn</th></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="../../lessons/5.1a.html">5.1a</a></td><td>The Axe: Item Pickups &amp; Inventory Bools</td><td>capability items — a pickup that is not a number</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="../../lessons/5.2a.html">5.2a</a></td><td>The Door: Breaking In with Events</td><td>Lever.Interact, UnityEvents, rules changed with no code</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="../../lessons/5.3a.html">5.3a</a></td><td>Into the Cave: Scene Loading &amp; Mode Switch</td><td>LoadScene, the scene-name branch, gravityScale flip</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Concepts this unit owns</h2>
<p>Three ideas the exam loves: <strong>right-sizing data</strong> (bool vs counter vs list), <strong>abstraction through events</strong> (the lever never names the door), and <strong>instance vs persistent state</strong> (what survives a scene load). CED: AAP-2 (managing complexity with abstraction — the UnityEvent), AAP-3, CRD-2.</p>

